# {{.Repo}} issues

The `issues/` directory next to this file holds a local copy of the {{.Scope}} in [{{.Repo}}](https://github.com/{{.Repo}}), one markdown file per issue, named `<number>.md`. Pull requests are not included. Paths below are relative to this skill's directory.

## Refresh first

The copy is only as recent as its last sync. Before searching, refresh it (this fetches only the issues updated since the last sync):

```
{{.Command}} -out <this skill's directory>
```

If `gh-issues-skill` is not installed or the sync fails, search the existing copy and mention that it may be out of date.

## File format

```
---
number: 123
title: "Crash when the config file is empty"
state: closed                 # open or closed
state_reason: completed       # if set: completed, not_planned, duplicate or reopened
labels: ["bug", "area/config"]
author: octocat
created: 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z
updated: 2026-01-02T00:00:00Z
closed: 2026-01-02T00:00:00Z  # closed issues only
url: https://github.com/{{.Repo}}/issues/123
---

# <title>

<body>

## Comments

### <author> at <time>

<comment>
```

## Finding related issues

1. Collect distinctive terms from the problem at hand: error messages, function, type and file names, component names, likely labels.
2. Search with grep, trying several terms and spellings. For example:
   - `grep -ril 'connection reset' issues/`
   - `grep -l '^state: open' issues/*.md`
   - `grep -l '"area/config"' issues/*.md`
3. Read the promising candidates in full, including comments. Related issues and duplicates are often linked there as `#123` or by URL; follow those links too.
4. Report each related issue by number and URL, whether it is open or closed, and a one-line reason why it is related.
